Subject: [PATCH 02633/11362] [outer-loop] Config Layer B move #1: simpler game
 (ka59) -- local gemma-12B CANNOT read raw 64x64 scene at either rule
 difficulty

Operator move #1: test whether the offline local 12B grounds a config win-rule on a SIMPLER game
(ka59, 1-D recolor strip) before concluding the model is too weak -- tr87 (hardest, glyph-rewrite)
proposed-but-didn't-ground. Same reader + same prompt scaffolding; only the game (rule difficulty)
changes.

Result: NOT grounded -- and the failure is more fundamental than tr87's. gemma-4-12B-it-Q4 on the
iGPU (offline-legal, ~4.2 tok/s) wrote 'def is_win(grid):' then filled the ENTIRE body with degenerate
repeated comments ('Row 63 is the bottom row.' / 'The rest of grid is the rule.' looping), never wrote
one line of logic, ran out of bounded tokens with an empty body -> syntax error. raw_sample banked in
the artifact as proof.

Conclusion: the bottleneck is RAW-SCENE READING, not rule complexity. The local 12B loses spatial
track of the dense 64x64 ASCII grid and degenerates -- on BOTH the hard rewrite (tr87) and the simple
recolor strip (ka59). So the offline model at this size/quant is too weak for raw-scene rule induction.
The lever is now clear: scaffolding that REMOVES the grid-parsing burden (hand the model the editable
region + candidate reference/legend regions as small structured arrays, not 64x64 ASCII), or a stronger
reader. NOT a simpler game.

Infra notes captured: iGPU throughput 4.2 tok/s (4096-token default needs ~975s > 900s timeout -> the
original TimeoutError); a stop:['python first; the propose-
then-ground verifier + bounded generation both work correctly.

Generalized scripts/experiments/arc3_config_layerb_llm.py (parameterized game, action-aware collector,
bounded generation w/ raw capture). Adversarial-verify clean (0 flags).

